SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Certificate Pinning and What It Costs',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains certificate pinning, and what it costs, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] A phone trusts dozens of certificate authorities, and a company proxy or malware can add one more. [[slnc 300]] Pinning makes an app trust one key it chose, instead of every authority on the device. [[slnc 300]] It stops that kind of interception, and it locks every copy of the app out on the day the server's key changes, unless that day was planned for. [[slnc 600]] Think of a door that opens for one key only. [[slnc 300]] Safer than a master key, until you have to change the lock. [[slnc 600]] Our example is a mobile banking app."),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['A mobile banking app talks to', 'api.bank.example.', '', 'The demo builds real certificate chains:', "  the bank's, from a public root", "  an interceptor's, from a root", '  a company proxy added to the phone', '', 'and tries several pin policies.'],
         narration="Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] A mobile banking app talks to the bank's A P I. [[slnc 500]] The demo builds real certificate chains. [[slnc 300]] One is the bank's, from a public root. [[slnc 300]] The other is an interceptor's, from a root that a company proxy added to the phone. [[slnc 500]] Then it runs the app's client with different pin policies, and counts what happens to ten thousand installed copies when the bank changes its key."),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Root: an authority the phone already trusts', 'Chain: leaf, then issuer, up to a root', 'Public key: the half of a key pair anyone may see', 'Pin: the hash of one public key, shipped in the app', 'Rotation: the day a server changes its key'],
         narration="Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] A root is an authority that the phone already trusts, kept in its own list. [[slnc 500]] A chain is the server's certificate, the leaf, then the issuer that signed it, up to a root. [[slnc 500]] A public key is the half of a key pair that anyone may see. [[slnc 500]] A pin is the hash of one public key, shipped inside the app. [[slnc 500]] And rotation is the day a server changes its key."),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['TLS: Transport Layer Security: the encrypted, checked connection under https', 'CA: Certificate Authority: signs certificates that clients trust', 'SPKI: SubjectPublicKeyInfo: a public key with the name of its algorithm, as bytes', 'SHA, SHA-256: Secure Hash Algorithm; SHA-256 gives 256 bits, 32 bytes', 'EC, P-256: Elliptic Curve keys, on the curve named P-256', 'HPKP: HTTP Public Key Pinning: pins set by a server header, now removed'],
         narration="A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] T L S is transport layer security: the encrypted and checked connection that https runs on. [[slnc 400]] C A is a certificate authority: it signs certificates that clients trust. [[slnc 400]] S P K I is subject public key info: a public key, with the name of its algorithm, as bytes. [[slnc 400]] S H A is the secure hash algorithm. S H A two fifty six gives two hundred and fifty six bits, thirty two bytes. [[slnc 400]] E C means elliptic curve, and P two fifty six is the name of the curve. [[slnc 400]] H P K P is H T T P public key pinning: pins set by a server's header, now removed from browsers."),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['HTTP: Hypertext Transfer Protocol: the requests and responses of the web', 'API: Application Programming Interface: the server the app talks to', 'JDK: Java Development Kit', 'PKIX: Public Key Infrastructure with X.509 certificates: the standard chain check', 'SSL: Secure Sockets Layer: the old name of TLS', 'Base64: a way to write bytes as letters: 64 symbols, 6 bits each'],
         narration='H T T P is the hypertext transfer protocol: the requests and responses of the web. [[slnc 400]] A P I is an application programming interface: here, the server the app talks to. [[slnc 400]] J D K is the Java development kit. [[slnc 400]] P K I X is public key infrastructure with X five oh nine certificates: the standard chain check. [[slnc 400]] S S L is secure sockets layer, the old name of T L S. [[slnc 400]] Base sixty four writes bytes as letters: sixty four symbols, six bits each.'),
    dict(key='06-trust-vs-pin', kind='figure', title='Trust the phone, or pin a key',
         body='trust_vs_pin',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one picture. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the two chains the banking app can receive. [[slnc 300]] The genuine one, from the bank's issuer. [[slnc 300]] And the interceptor's, for the very same name, from a proxy's issuer. [[slnc 700]] On the left, an app that trusts the phone. [[slnc 300]] The proxy's root was added to the phone, so both chains are valid, and both are accepted. [[slnc 700]] On the right, an app that pins the key of the bank's leaf. [[slnc 300]] The genuine chain holds that key, and is accepted. [[slnc 300]] The interceptor's chain does not, and is refused by the pin. [[slnc 700]] Every value here comes from the demo's real output. [[slnc 300]] The rest of the video is about what that pin costs."),
    dict(key='07-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='Every root on the phone can vouch',
         body='device_roots',
         narration="First demo: an app that trusts the device. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the roots the phone trusts. [[slnc 300]] Public Root C A came with the phone. [[slnc 300]] Helpful Proxy Root was added later, by a company proxy, a debugging tool, or malware. [[slnc 700]] Below, the two chains the app might receive. [[slnc 300]] The genuine one: api dot bank dot example, signed by Public Issuing C A. [[slnc 300]] The interceptor's: the same name, signed by Helpful Proxy C A. [[slnc 700]] In the middle, the check done by hand. [[slnc 300]] Walk to the top of the chain, and ask: which root on the phone signed it? [[slnc 300]] The genuine chain ends at Public Root C A. [[slnc 300]] The interceptor's ends at Helpful Proxy Root. [[slnc 300]] Both are on the phone, so both pass, and the app accepts both. [[slnc 300]] The proxy reads every request, password and all."),
    dict(key='08-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — An app that trusts the device',
         body="ONE. An app that trusts the device.\n  THE DEVICE'S ROOTS (what the phone trusts):\n    Public Root CA      shipped with the phone\n    Helpful Proxy Root  added by a proxy, a tool or malware\n  THE CHAINS (leaf first, as each server sends it):\n    genuine       api.bank.example <- Public Issuing CA\n    interception  api.bank.example <- Helpful Proxy CA",
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The two roots on the phone, and the two chains, leaf first.'),
    dict(key='09-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — An app that trusts the device',
         body='ONE. (continued)\n  THE CHECK, BY HAND (signed by a root on the phone?):\n    genuine       yes, by Public Root CA\n    interception  yes, by Helpful Proxy Root\n  THE RESULT (the app has no pins):\n    genuine       ACCEPTED\n    interception  ACCEPTED  <- THE PROXY READS IT ALL',
         narration='The check by hand: each chain is signed by a root on the phone. [[slnc 400]] And the result, with no pins: both accepted. [[slnc 300]] The proxy reads it all.'),
    dict(key='10-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='How a pin is made',
         body='pin_worked_out',
         narration="Second demo: an app that pins the server's key. [[slnc 400]] First, what a pin is, worked out with the real values. [[slnc 700]] The input is the leaf's public key, in a standard form called subject public key info. [[slnc 300]] Ninety one bytes: twenty six bytes of header, which name the algorithm and the curve, and the sixty five byte point itself. [[slnc 600]] Those bytes go through S H A two fifty six, which gives thirty two bytes. [[slnc 300]] And the thirty two bytes are written in base sixty four, which gives forty four characters. [[slnc 300]] That is the pin. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, base sixty four by hand. [[slnc 300]] Take three bytes: two f, three a, b f. [[slnc 300]] That is twenty four bits. [[slnc 300]] Cut them into four groups of six bits, which are eleven, fifty one, forty two and sixty three. [[slnc 300]] In the base sixty four alphabet, those are capital L, small z, small q, and slash. [[slnc 300]] The first four letters of the pin. [[slnc 600]] Thirty two bytes make ten groups of three, with two left over, so eleven groups of four letters: forty four."),
    dict(key='11-act-2-1', kind='console', title="Act Two — An app that pins the server's key",
         body="TWO. An app that pins the server's key.\n  THE INPUT (the leaf's public key, EC P-256):\n    SubjectPublicKeyInfo  91 bytes = 26 header + 65 point\n  THE PIN, WORKED OUT:\n    SHA-256 of the 91 bytes  32 bytes  2f3abf264f56...\n    Base64 of the hash  44 chars, the pin:\n      Lzq/Jk9WKkUdfAIkFfeRw+9Of4eKCEcGlfwZSm7fewM=",
         narration='The output. [[slnc 400]] Ninety one bytes of key. [[slnc 300]] Thirty two bytes of hash. [[slnc 300]] Forty four characters of pin.'),
    dict(key='12-act-2-2', kind='console', title="Act Two — An app that pins the server's key",
         body="TWO. (continued)\n  THE BASE64, BY HAND (3 bytes -> 4 letters of 6 bits):\n    bytes    2f 3a bf\n    6 bits   001011 110011 101010 111111\n    values   11     51     42     63\n    letters  L      z      q      /\n    32 bytes = 10 x 3 + 2 left -> 11 groups x 4 = 44 chars\n    same as the JDK's Base64: yes",
         narration="The first group by hand: three bytes, four groups of six bits, four letters. [[slnc 400]] The same as the J D K's base sixty four."),
    dict(key='13-act-2-3', kind='console', title="Act Two — An app that pins the server's key",
         body="TWO. (continued)\n  THE CHECK, BY HAND (is a key of the chain pinned?):\n    genuine       yes, the leaf key Lzq/Jk9WKkUd...\n    interception  none of 2 keys\n  THE RESULT (the app ships the leaf's pin):\n    genuine                    ACCEPTED\n    interception               REFUSED_BY_PIN\n    interception, clean phone  REFUSED_BY_VALIDATION",
         narration="Now the check by hand. [[slnc 300]] The genuine chain holds the pinned leaf key. [[slnc 300]] The interceptor's chain holds none. [[slnc 400]] So the app accepts the bank, and refuses the interceptor by pin. [[slnc 300]] On a clean phone, validation refuses it first."),
    dict(key='14-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='The day the key changes',
         body='renewal_outage',
         narration='Third demo: the bank renews its certificate. [[slnc 400]] Day eighty, a new certificate, and a new key, as many renewals do. [[slnc 700]] At the top, the pin the app ships, and the pin of the new key. [[slnc 300]] They are different. [[slnc 300]] So none of the keys in the new chain is pinned, and the app refuses its own bank. [[slnc 300]] Nothing is wrong with the certificate. [[slnc 300]] The pin did exactly what it was told. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, what that means for the bank. [[slnc 300]] Ten thousand installed copies of the app. [[slnc 300]] On day zero, all ten thousand fail at once. [[slnc 300]] Say eighty five percent update within a week. [[slnc 300]] Ten thousand, minus ten thousand times eighty five over a hundred, leaves one thousand five hundred customers who still cannot log in, a week later.'),
    dict(key='15-act-3', kind='console', title='Act Three — The server renews with a new key',
         body="THREE. The server renews with a new key.\n  THE RENEWAL (day 80: a new certificate, a new key):\n    the app's pin      Lzq/Jk9WKkUd...\n    the new key's pin  ZDwOX/eMCYca...\n  THE CHECK, BY HAND (is a key of the chain pinned?):\n    renewed chain     none of 2 keys\n  THE RESULT (the app pinned to the old leaf key):\n    renewed chain     REFUSED_BY_PIN  <- THE BANK'S OWN APP\n  THE FLEET, WORKED OUT (10000 installs, 85% update in a week):\n    locked out on day 0   10000\n    after a week          10000 - 10000 x 85 / 100 = 1500",
         narration="The output. [[slnc 400]] The app's pin, and the new key's pin, are different. [[slnc 300]] By hand, none of the renewed chain's keys is pinned. [[slnc 300]] So the bank's own app refuses the bank. [[slnc 400]] And the fleet: ten thousand fail on day zero, fifteen hundred still fail a week later."),
    dict(key='16-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='Pins that survive a renewal',
         body='rotation_pins',
         narration="Fourth demo: pins chosen so that a key change does not break the app. [[slnc 400]] Three choices, each checked by hand. [[slnc 700]] First, pin the issuing authority's key instead of the leaf. [[slnc 300]] The renewal changed the leaf, not the issuer, so the renewed chain still holds a pinned key, and it is accepted. [[slnc 300]] And the interceptor's chain still holds none, so it is still refused. [[slnc 700]] Second, pin the leaf and a backup key, made in advance and kept offline. [[slnc 300]] When the server switches to the backup key, the chain holds a pinned key, and it is accepted. [[slnc 700]] Third, report only. [[slnc 300]] No key matches, but the app accepts, and sends one report. [[slnc 300]] The team learns what would have broken, before it breaks."),
    dict(key='17-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — Pins chosen so that rotation does not break the app',
         body="FOUR. Pins chosen so that rotation does not break the app.\n  THE PINS (what each app ships):\n    issuer pin     the Public Issuing CA's key eb8u2CB5PRIO...\n    leaf + backup  the leaf key, and a spare kept offline\n    report-only    the leaf key; a mismatch is only reported\n  THE CHECK, BY HAND (which key of the chain is pinned?):\n    issuer pin, renewed       yes, the issuer's key eb8u2CB5PRIO...\n    issuer pin, interception  none of 2 keys\n    backup pin, key switched  yes, the backup key iJUyqyKML7H6...\n    report-only, renewed      none of 2 keys",
         narration='The output. [[slnc 400]] The three pin choices, and the check by hand: which key of each chain is pinned.'),
    dict(key='18-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — Pins chosen so that rotation does not break the app',
         body='FOUR. (continued)\n  THE RESULT:\n    issuer pin, renewed       ACCEPTED\n    issuer pin, interception  REFUSED_BY_PIN\n    backup pin, key switched  ACCEPTED\n    report-only, renewed      ACCEPTED_REPORT_ONLY\n    reports collected         1',
         narration='And the results. [[slnc 300]] The issuer pin accepts the renewal and refuses the interceptor. [[slnc 300]] The backup pin accepts the switched key. [[slnc 300]] Report only accepts, and collects one report.'),
    dict(key='19-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='One header, a year of lockout',
         body='hpkp_year',
         narration="Fifth demo: why browsers stopped letting servers set pins. [[slnc 400]] For a few years, a server could send pins in a header, called H P K P, and the browser kept them. [[slnc 700]] At the top, the header a meddler sends, when it answers one first visit. [[slnc 300]] Two pins, both for the meddler's own keys, and a max age. [[slnc 600]] The max age, worked out. [[slnc 300]] Thirty one million, five hundred and thirty six thousand seconds, divided by eighty six thousand four hundred seconds in a day, is three hundred and sixty five days. [[slnc 700]] On the timeline, the genuine bank server. [[slnc 300]] Day thirty is less than three hundred and sixty five, so the meddler's pins still apply, and every returning visitor is blocked from the real bank. [[slnc 300]] Day four hundred is past the expiry, and the bank is accepted again. [[slnc 600]] A site could do the same to itself, by pinning its own keys and then losing them. [[slnc 300]] That is why browsers removed the feature."),
    dict(key='20-act-5-1', kind='console', title='Act Five — Why browsers stopped letting servers set pins',
         body='FIVE. Why browsers stopped letting servers set pins.\n  THE HEADER (a meddler answers one first visit, HPKP):\n    Public-Key-Pins: pin-sha256="uY0GG0fH8UUc...";\n      pin-sha256="wwq5cMyuB4F8..."; max-age=31536000\n  THE max-age, WORKED OUT:\n    31536000 s / 86400 s a day = 365 days',
         narration="The output. [[slnc 400]] The meddler's header, with a max age of three hundred and sixty five days."),
    dict(key='21-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — Why browsers stopped letting servers set pins',
         body="FIVE. (continued)\n  THE CHECK, BY HAND (is the visit within 365 days?):\n    day 30   30 < 365: the meddler's pins still apply\n    day 400  400 > 365: the pins have expired\n  THE RESULT (the genuine bank server):\n    day 30   blocked for every returning visitor\n    day 400  accepted again\n  THE SITE'S OWN PINS (leaf + backup), THEN A NEW KEY:\n    day 80   blocked until day 365  <- NO WAY TO UNDO IT",
         narration='By hand: day thirty is inside the year, day four hundred is outside. [[slnc 400]] The result agrees: blocked on day thirty, accepted on day four hundred. [[slnc 400]] And a site that pins its own keys, then changes key, is blocked until day three hundred and sixty five.'),
    dict(key='22-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['Changing the key becomes a project', 'A backup pin needs a real, unused key', 'Pinning an issuing CA trusts all it signs', 'Customers cannot work around a pin', 'Testing tools need pins off in debug builds'],
         narration="Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] Changing the server's key becomes a project, planned months ahead. [[slnc 500]] A backup pin needs a real key, made in advance, kept offline, and not yet in use. [[slnc 500]] Pinning an issuing authority's key trusts everything that authority signs. [[slnc 500]] A customer who is locked out cannot work around a pin. [[slnc 300]] And testing with interception tools needs pins switched off in debug builds only."),
    dict(key='23-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['"SSL pinning bypass" in a mobile test report', 'A pin set in an Android network config', 'An app outage after a certificate renewal', 'Public-Key-Pins headers in old captures'],
         narration="You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] S S L pinning bypass, in a mobile security test report, is a tester removing the pin to watch the traffic. [[slnc 500]] A pin set in an Android network security configuration is this check, written as settings. [[slnc 500]] An app that stopped working after the server's certificate was renewed is the third demo. [[slnc 500]] And a public key pins header, in an old capture, is the browser feature that was removed."),
    dict(key='24-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Pin only where the threat is real', '  and you control the updates', 'Pin the issuing CA, or the leaf plus a backup', 'Report-only first; rehearse the rotation', 'Keep a way to update every customer', 'For websites: Certificate Transparency, not pins'],
         narration="So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Pin only in apps where interception is a real threat, and where you control the updates. [[slnc 500]] Pin the issuing authority's key, or the leaf together with a backup key. [[slnc 500]] Run in report only mode first, and rehearse the key change. [[slnc 300]] Keep a way to update every customer before the old key goes. [[slnc 500]] And for websites, never let servers set pins. [[slnc 300]] Certificate Transparency watches for wrong certificates without locking anyone out."),
    dict(key='25-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['PinnedClient: validates the chain like any client, then checks the pins', "Spki: the pin: SHA-256 of the key's bytes, in Base64", 'PinSteps: the same pin, worked out step by step', 'ChainWalk: the two checks, redone by hand', 'PinStore: pins set by a server header, as old browsers kept them'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] A client that validates and then checks pins, the pin itself, two small classes that redo the checks by hand, and the old header store. [[slnc 300]] Here they are.'),
    dict(key='26-class-1', kind='code', title='PinnedClient — validation first, then the pin',
         body='// PinnedClient: validates the chain like any client, then checks the pins\nif (!validates(chain, host, now)) {\n    return Outcome.REFUSED_BY_VALIDATION;\n}\n...\nboolean pinned = chain.stream().anyMatch(c -> policy.pins().contains(Spki.pin(c)));\n...\nreturn policy.reportOnly() ? Outcome.ACCEPTED_REPORT_ONLY : Outcome.REFUSED_BY_PIN;',
         narration="Validation runs first, with the J D K's P K I X validator, so a pin can never make a bad chain good. [[slnc 300]] Then at least one key of the chain must be pinned. [[slnc 300]] In report only mode, a mismatch is recorded, and the connection goes on."),
    dict(key='27-class-2', kind='code', title='Spki — hash the key, then Base64',
         body='// Spki: the pin: SHA-256 of the key\'s bytes, in Base64\nbyte[] hash = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256").digest(key.getEncoded());\nreturn Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(hash);',
         narration='The encoded public key is the subject public key info. [[slnc 300]] Its S H A two fifty six hash, in base sixty four, is the pin, the same form that Android and OkHttp use.'),
    dict(key='28-class-3', kind='code', title='PinSteps — Base64 by hand',
         body="// PinSteps: the same pin, worked out step by step\nfor (int i = 0; i < bytes.length; i += 3) {\n...\n        bits = bits << 8 | (j < n ? bytes[i + j] & 0xff : 0);\n...\n        out.append(k <= n ? ALPHABET.charAt(bits >> 18 - 6 * k & 0x3f) : '=');",
         narration='Three bytes are packed into twenty four bits. [[slnc 300]] Each six bits pick one letter of the alphabet. [[slnc 300]] A short last group is padded with an equals sign. [[slnc 300]] A test proves it matches the J D K for every length.'),
    dict(key='29-class-4', kind='code', title='ChainWalk — which root signed it, which key is pinned',
         body='// ChainWalk: the two checks, redone by hand\nboolean named = root.getSubjectX500Principal().equals(top.getIssuerX500Principal());\nif (named && signedBy(top, root)) {\n    return Optional.of(root);\n}\n...\nreturn chain.stream().filter(c -> pins.contains(Spki.pin(c))).findFirst();',
         narration="A root anchors the chain when its name is the top certificate's issuer, and its key verifies the signature. [[slnc 300]] The pin check looks for the first key of the chain whose pin the app ships."),
    dict(key='30-class-5', kind='code', title='PinStore — remembered until max-age runs out',
         body='// PinStore: pins set by a server header, as old browsers kept them\nentries.put(host, new Entry(pins, now.plus(Duration.ofSeconds(maxAge))));\n...\nif (e == null || now.isAfter(e.expires())) {\n    return true;\n}',
         narration='The store keeps the pins of a header until its max age has passed. [[slnc 300]] Until then, a chain with none of those keys is blocked, whoever sent the header.'),
    dict(key='31-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add an enforcement date', 'to a pin set; stop enforcing it once it passes.'],
         narration="That's certificate pinning. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] A pin narrows trust from every authority on the device to a key you chose, so it stops interception by an unexpected root, and it costs a planned key change, a backup pin and a way to update. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Give a pin set an expiry date, and stop enforcing it once that date has passed without an update. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

